viyogaṁ yoga-saṁjñitam\"}]},{\"section\":\"synonyms\",\"section_title\":\"Books.Verse.synonyms\",\"children\":[{\"type\":\"books.Synonyms\",\"title\":\"synonyms\",\"id\":1891,\"bb\":1672,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1672/\",\"shadow_value\":\"$25\",\"word_for_word_value\":[{\"word\":\"yatra\",\"translation\":\"in that state of affairs where\"},{\"word\":\"uparamate\",\"translation\":\"cease (because one feels transcendental happiness)\"},{\"word\":\"cittam\",\"translation\":\"mental activities\"},{\"word\":\"niruddham\",\"translation\":\"being restrained from matter\"},{\"word\":\"yoga-sevayā\",\"translation\":\"by performance of yoga\"},{\"word\":\"yatra\",\"translation\":\"in which\"},{\"word\":\"ca\",\"translation\":\"also\"},{\"word\":\"eva\",\"translation\":\"certainly\"},{\"word\":\"ātmanā\",\"translation\":\"by the pure mind\"},{\"word\":\"ātmānam\",\"translation\":\"the Self\"},{\"word\":\"paśyan\",\"translation\":\"realizing the position of\"},{\"word\":\"ātmani\",\"translation\":\"in the Self\"},{\"word\":\"tuṣyati\",\"translation\":\"one becomes satisfied\"},{\"word\":\"sukham\",\"translation\":\"happiness\"},{\"word\":\"ātyantikam\",\"translation\":\"supreme\"},{\"word\":\"yat\",\"translation\":\"which\"},{\"word\":\"tat\",\"translation\":\"that\"},{\"word\":\"buddhi\",\"translation\":\"by intelligence\"},{\"word\":\"grāhyam\",\"translation\":\"accessible\"},{\"word\":\"atīndriyam\",\"translation\":\"transcendental\"},{\"word\":\"vetti\",\"translation\":\"one knows\"},{\"word\":\"yatra\",\"translation\":\"wherein\"},{\"word\":\"na\",\"translation\":\"never\"},{\"word\":\"ca\",\"translation\":\"also\"},{\"word\":\"eva\",\"translation\":\"certainly\"},{\"word\":\"ayam\",\"translation\":\"he\"},{\"word\":\"sthitaḥ\",\"translation\":\"situated\"},{\"word\":\"calati\",\"translation\":\"moves\"},{\"word\":\"tattvataḥ\",\"translation\":\"from the truth\"},{\"word\":\"yam\",\"translation\":\"that which\"},{\"word\":\"labdhvā\",\"translation\":\"by attainment\"},{\"word\":\"ca\",\"translation\":\"also\"},{\"word\":\"aparam\",\"translation\":\"any other\"},{\"word\":\"lābham\",\"translation\":\"gain\"},{\"word\":\"manyate\",\"translation\":\"considers\"},{\"word\":\"na\",\"translation\":\"never\"},{\"word\":\"adhikam\",\"translation\":\"more\"},{\"word\":\"tataḥ\",\"translation\":\"than that\"},{\"word\":\"yasmin\",\"translation\":\"in which\"},{\"word\":\"sthitaḥ\",\"translation\":\"being situated\"},{\"word\":\"na\",\"translation\":\"never\"},{\"word\":\"duḥkhena\",\"translation\":\"by miseries\"},{\"word\":\"guruṇā api\",\"translation\":\"even though very difficult\"},{\"word\":\"vicālyate\",\"translation\":\"becomes shaken\"},{\"word\":\"tam\",\"translation\":\"that\"},{\"word\":\"vidyāt\",\"translation\":\"you must know\"},{\"word\":\"duḥkha-saṁyoga\",\"translation\":\"of the miseries of material contact\"},{\"word\":\"viyogam\",\"translation\":\"extermination\"},{\"word\":\"yoga-saṁjñitam\",\"translation\":\"called trance in yoga.\"}]}]},{\"section\":\"translation\",\"section_title\":\"Books.Verse.translation\",\"children\":[{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"translation\",\"id\":1892,\"bb\":1673,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1673/\",\"body_value\":\"In the stage of perfection called trance, or samādhi, one’s mind is completely restrained from material mental activities by practice of yoga. This perfection is characterized by one’s ability to see the Self by the pure mind and to relish and rejoice in the Self. In that joyous state, one is situated in boundless transcendental happiness, realized through transcendental senses. Established thus, one never departs from the truth, and upon gaining this he thinks there is no greater gain. Being situated in such a position, one is never shaken, even in the midst of greatest difficulty. This indeed is actual freedom from all miseries arising from material contact.\"}]},{\"section\":\"purport\",\"section_title\":\"Books.Verse.purport\",\"children\":[{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":1893,\"bb\":1674,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1674/\",\"body_value\":\"$26\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":1894,\"bb\":1675,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1675/\",\"body_value\":\"This citi-śakti, or internal potency, is transcendental. Puruṣārtha means material religiosity, economic development, sense gratification and, at the end, the attempt to become one with the Supreme. This “oneness with the Supreme” is called kaivalyam by the monist. But according to Patañjali, this kaivalyam is an internal, or transcendental, potency by which the living entity becomes aware of his constitutional position. In the words of Lord Caitanya, this state of affairs is called ceto-darpaṇa-mārjanam, or clearance of the impure mirror of the mind. This “clearance” is actually liberation, or bhava-mahā-dāvāgni-nirvāpaṇam. The theory of nirvāṇa – also preliminary – corresponds with this principle. In the Bhāgavatam (2.10.6) this is called svarūpeṇa vyavasthitiḥ. The Bhagavad-gītā also confirms this situation in this verse.\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":1895,\"bb\":1676,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1676/\",\"body_value\":\"After nirvāṇa, or material cessation, there is the manifestation of spiritual activities, or devotional service to the Lord, known as Kṛṣṇa consciousness. In the words of the Bhāgavatam, svarūpeṇa vyavasthitiḥ: this is the “real life of the living entity.” Māyā, or illusion, is the condition of spiritual life contaminated by material infection. Liberation from this material infection does not mean destruction of the original eternal position of the living entity. Patañjali also accepts this by his words kaivalyaṁ svarūpa-pratiṣṭhā vā citi-śaktir iti. This citi-śakti, or transcendental pleasure, is real life. This is confirmed in the Vedānta-sūtra (1.1.12) as ānanda-mayo ’bhyāsāt. This natural transcendental pleasure is the ultimate goal of yoga and is easily achieved by execution of devotional service, or bhakti-yoga. Bhakti-yoga will be vividly described in the Seventh Chapter of Bhagavad-gītā.\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":1896,\"bb\":1677,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1677/\",\"body_value\":\"$27\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":1897,\"bb\":1678,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1678/\",\"body_value\":\"The best practice of yoga in this age is Kṛṣṇa consciousness, which is not baffling. A Kṛṣṇa conscious person is so happy in his occupation that he does not aspire after any other happiness. There are many impediments, especially in this age of hypocrisy, to practicing haṭha-yoga, dhyāna-yoga and jñāna-yoga, but there is no such problem in executing karma-yoga or bhakti-yoga.\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":1898,\"bb\":1679,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/6/20-23/b1679/\",\"body_value\":\"As long as the material body exists, one has to meet the demands of the body, namely eating, sleeping, defending and mating. But a person who is in pure bhakti-yoga, or in Kṛṣṇa consciousness, does not arouse the senses while meeting the demands of the body. Rather, he accepts the bare necessities of life, making the best use of a bad bargain, and enjoys transcendental happiness in Kṛṣṇa consciousness. He is callous toward incidental occurrences – such as accidents, disease, scarcity and even the death of a most dear relative – but he is always alert to execute his duties in Kṛṣṇa consciousness, or bhakti-yoga. Accidents never deviate him from his duty. As stated in the Bhagavad-gītā (2.14), āgamāpāyino ’nityās tāṁs titikṣasva bhārata. He endures all such incidental occurrences because he knows that they come and go and do not affect his duties. In this way he achieves the highest perfection in yoga practice.\"}]}],\"short_title\":\"Bg. 6.20-23\",\"medium_title\":\"Bhagavad-gītā As It Is 6.20-23\"},\"locale\":\"en\",\"activeView\":\"$6:props:activeView\"}]}],\"$L28\",\"$L29\",\"$L2a\"]}]\n"])self.__next_f.push([1,"2c:I[83068,[\"/_next/static/chunks/130enlymdm.jk.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0b6f_zxj9ay~6.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/143cg6c13uly6.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/07vk.k35-e~ti.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0j6k6n9cniknh.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0.zxv9arc6a56.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/00-zmo-3n1bt8.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0p4z5tusn0jt6.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/01mcazkjru_is.js\"],\"default\"]\n32:I[78455,[\"/_next/static/chunks/130enlymdm.jk.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0b6f_zxj9ay~6.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/143cg6c13uly6.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/07vk.k35-e~ti.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0j6k6n9cniknh.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0.zxv9arc6a56.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/00-zmo-3n1bt8.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0p4z5tusn0jt6.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/01mcazkjru_is.js\"],\"default\"]\n28:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"px-4 py-4 w-full max-w-screen-xl mx-auto md:px-8 mb-3\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"nav\",null,{\"aria-label\":\"Breadcrumb\",\"className\":\"breadcrumb mb-8 text-base\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"ol\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"/en/library/\",{\"className\":\"inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L20\",null,{\"href\":\"/en/library/\",\"className\":\"text-vb-link\",\"children\":\"Library\"}],\" » \"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"/en/library/bg/\",{\"className\":\"inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L20\",null,{\"href\":\"/en/library/bg/\",\"className\":\"text-vb-link\",\"children\":\"Bhagavad-gītā As It Is\"}],\" » \"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"/en/library/bg/6/\",{\"className\":\"inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L20\",null,{\"href\":\"/en/library/bg/6/\",\"className\":\"text-vb-link\",\"children\":\"Chapter Six: Dhyāna-yoga\"}],false]}]]}]}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"children\":[\"$L2b\",[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2c\",null,{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page\",\"hasId\":false,\"isWholePage\":true,